format: equavela-worksheet/3
page:
  size: A4
  margins: {top: 54, right: 54, bottom: 54, left: 54}
settings:
  format.precision: 5
regions:
  - id: title
    page: 1
    at: [54, 54]
    text:
      width: full
      text: |
        {style=title}Fourier series and the Gibbs phenomenon

        A square wave of period $2·π$ is approximated by the partial sums of its Fourier series. The sums converge to the wave wherever it is continuous, yet next to each jump they overshoot by a fixed fraction of the jump, however many terms are added: the **Gibbs phenomenon**.
  - id: h1
    page: 1
    at: [54, 182]
    text: "{style=heading1}The series"
  - id: t1
    page: 1
    at: [54, 208]
    text:
      width: full
      text: The wave is odd, so only sine terms appear. The coefficient of $sin(n·x)$ is one integral over half a period, solved symbolically for an integer $n > 0$.
  - id: b
    page: 1
    at: [54, 236]
    math: "2/π·∫[x ∈ 0..π] sin(n·x) →[assume, n == integer, n > 0][simplify]"
  - id: t2
    page: 1
    at: [54, 274]
    text:
      width: full
      text: As $(−1)^n$ is $−1$ for odd $n$ and $1$ for even $n$, the coefficient is $4/(π·n)$ for odd $n$ and $0$ for even $n$. The partial sum with the first $N$ odd harmonics is
  - id: S
    page: 1
    at: [54, 318]
    math: "S(N, x) := 4/π·∑[k ∈ 1..N] sin((2·k − 1)·x)/(2·k − 1)"
  - id: t3
    page: 1
    at: [54, 366]
    text:
      width: full
      text: Two partial sums over one period, against the wave itself. With more terms the sum follows the wave more closely, but the spike next to each jump keeps its height and only gets narrower.
  - id: x
    page: 1
    at: [54, 400]
    math: "x := 0, 0.004..2·π"
  - id: plot
    page: 1
    at: [54, 422]
    plot:
      version: 1
      type: xy
      size: [487, 165]
      axes:
        y: {title: "Partial sums"}
      traces:
        - {id: wave, x: "x", y: "sign(sin(x))", show_in_legend: true, legend_label: "square wave", line: {color: "#1B2A3A", width: 1}}
        - {id: s3, x: "x", y: "S(3, x)", show_in_legend: true, legend_label: "N = 3", line: {color: "#01B5AF"}}
        - {id: s25, x: "x", y: "S(25, x)", show_in_legend: true, legend_label: "N = 25", line: {color: "#00428D"}}
  - id: h2
    page: 1
    at: [54, 594]
    text: "{style=heading1}The overshoot does not vanish"
  - id: t4
    page: 1
    at: [54, 620]
    text:
      width: full
      text: After the jump at the origin, the first maximum of $S(N, x)$ lies at $π/(2·N)$. Its height tends to a constant above $1$, so the sums overshoot by about $9 %$ of the jump from $−1$ to $1$ for every $N$.
  - id: G
    page: 1
    at: [54, 654]
    math: "G(N) := S(N, π/(2·N))"
  - id: G10
    page: 1
    at: [54, 686]
    math: "G(10) = "
  - id: G100
    page: 1
    at: [210, 686]
    math: "G(100) = "
  - id: G1000
    page: 1
    at: [380, 686]
    math: "G(1000) = "
  - id: t5
    page: 1
    at: [54, 712]
    text:
      width: full
      text: The limit is the sine integral at $π$, scaled by $2/π$; its excess over $1$, taken relative to the jump of $2$, is the Wilbraham–Gibbs constant.
  - id: lim
    page: 1
    at: [54, 748]
    math: "G_lim := 2/π·∫[t ∈ 0..π] sin(t)/t"
  - id: limv
    page: 1
    at: [260, 748]
    math: "G_lim = "
  - id: over
    page: 1
    at: [400, 748]
    math: "(G_lim − 1)/2 = "
